{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent upward bias in realized prices vs model: during strong uptrends the model systematically under-forecasts the level (often by ~2–5 €/t, increasing with horizon). Apply a positive level shift that grows with forecast horizon when recent returns are positive and volatility is rising.",
    "Trend/lag error: the model reacts too slowly to regime changes (fails to carry momentum). If the last 5–10 days show a sustained climb, increase the slope of the forecast path (add a momentum term) rather than reverting toward the recent mean.",
    "Mean-reversion is too strong: forecasts pull back toward ~64–66 even when the market breaks out to ~68–70. Reduce mean-reversion weight when price is above the 20–30d mean AND the 10d trend is positive.",
    "Horizon-specific drift: short horizon (days 1–5) errors are small; medium/long horizon (days 10–30) errors skew increasingly negative. Use a horizon-dependent bias correction (e.g., +0.5 €/t by day 5, +2 €/t by day 15, +3–4 €/t by day 30 in bullish regimes).",
    "Volatility/convexity miss: after an upside breakout the true path accelerates more than the model (higher highs). When price makes a new 20–30d high, add an extra breakout premium for the next 1–3 weeks instead of flattening.",
    "Avoid dampening after spikes: the model tends to fade rallies too early. If price closes above prior resistance (recent local high), delay any reversion adjustment until at least 3–5 sessions of consolidation."
  ]
}
